rewritten-to-match
The graph has the reference's shape: a pool of vertex objects each with its own neighbour list, built by the reference's generator and random sequence. Fixed capacities where the reference's Vecs grow: 48 neighbours per vertex (the largest the generator makes for the run parameters is 47, measured by simulating it) and a queue array of one slot per vertex (the reference's VecDeque holds at most 914 at once). The per-run visited array is a fill of a state array. The BFS loop and its early return are state machines (a list is walked whole, so after the target is found the rest of that vertex's neighbours are skipped by a test, at most 47 cheap iterations once per run). Other stores are range-checked; state is unlogged, so nothing is saved for a rollback. Scratch state rebuilt every run (the reference's locals: visited, qv, qd) is not logged (state is unlogged by default, so no undo log, as a Rust local has none).
